+# The Daily Grind
+
+A static coffee homepage with a responsive My Account workspace. No packages or build step are required: `index.html`, `styles.css`, and `account.js` are the application.
+
+## Account features
+
+- Register with a name, email, and discoverable WebAuthn passkey.
+- Sign in with a saved passkey, optionally filtering by email.
+- Add, rename, and revoke saved passkeys; keep at least one to avoid lockout.
+- Update personal details after fresh passkey verification.
+- Reauthenticate explicitly, and sign out of the in-memory session.
+- Handle canceled prompts, unsupported browsers, duplicate accounts, storage failures, and changes from another tab.
+
+The browser uses `navigator.credentials.create/get` with user verification required and ES256 credentials. Assertions are checked against fresh random challenges, the exact origin, the relying-party ID hash, presence and verification flags, the account user handle, and the stored public key. ECDSA signatures are verified with Web Crypto. Private keys and biometric data are never read or stored by the app. Synced passkeys are supported; counters are not treated as monotonic because synced authenticators can report zero or non-monotonic counters.
+
+## Scope
+
+This repository has no backend. Account records and public keys are stored in localStorage, and the signed-in session exists only in memory. Refreshing signs out; clearing site data removes the local account. Email addresses identify local accounts and are not email-verified. Renaming an account or a passkey does not change its label in the system password manager. Revoking a passkey removes it from this application's allowlist, not from the authenticator.
+
+This is a working **browser-local implementation**, not a production authentication boundary: client code and localStorage are under the browser owner's control. Production customer accounts require a backend that owns registration/authentication challenges, verifies WebAuthn responses server-side, persists credentials, and issues secure HttpOnly session cookies. Cross-browser account access and recovery also require that backend. Do not use client-side identity checks to authorize server resources.
+
+WebAuthn requires HTTPS or localhost, a current supporting browser, and an authenticator capable of ES256 discoverable credentials and user verification. The browser must support `AuthenticatorAttestationResponse.getPublicKey()` and `getAuthenticatorData()`. Serve these static files from the same origin; passkeys are tied to its hostname. File URLs are not supported.
+
+## Validation
+
+`npm run check` checks JavaScript syntax without running the application or importing a server. `npm test` runs Node tests against mocked browser credential APIs and real Web Crypto signatures, covering registration, sign-in, credential management, profile changes, reauthentication, and rejection of invalid assertions. No build script is needed. Native authenticator prompts need separate end-to-end verification on a secure origin; they cannot be validated by a static syntax check.
